{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Us6GjKxnCwucCKSqNM5jVc","type":"tool_result","content":"total 241\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 Peter 197609     0 Oct 11 15:53 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 Peter 197609     0 Oct 11 14:59 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 Peter 197609 18201 Oct 11 14:59 001-REVIEW-A-290c777.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 Peter 197609 10312 Oct 11 15:34 002-REVIEW-A-9752b92-narrow.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 Peter 197609  9328 Oct 11 15:47 003-REVIEW-A-9be10a0-narrow.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 Peter 197609     0 Oct 11 15:53 004-ADJUDICATE-028ecf8.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 Peter 197609    68 Oct 11 15:53 004-ADJUDICATE-028ecf8.md.requested\nbrief = combined\nkinds = amendment;first-approval\noutcome = pending\n0 docs/reviews/wi-798/004-ADJUDICATE-028ecf8.md\n# docs/process.toml — HOW ITEMS ARE PROCESSED. Hand-edited, machine-read.\n#\n# One home for every POLICY DIAL (SN-028). Before this file the dials lived in\n# ~10 one-word files under docs/ (docs/gate-policy, docs/push-policy, …). That\n# idiom was never a ruling — it accreted, and the one constraint that really\n# held it in place was the git hooks' pure-sh parse (repo-review M-42,\n# 2026-07-21: a Python-less box must still FAIL CLOSED on a declared privacy\n# policy). A one-word-per-file format is the only thing a `head -n 1` parse can\n# read; the old 3.8 floor's lack of `tomllib` did the rest.\n#\n# Both reasons are spent. The floor is 3.11 (`tomllib` is stdlib), and the\n# hooks now do a KEYED match instead of a first-line one:\n#\n#     grep -Eq '^[[:space:]]*privacy_check[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*true' docs/process.toml\n#\n# which is why every value below is written ONE `key = value` PER LINE with no\n# inline tables and no multi-line strings. Python consumers read it with\n# `tomllib` and a cross-parser agreement test pins the two readings equal, so\n# the greppable form can never drift from the parsed one.\n#\n# THREE DELIBERATE EXCEPTIONS stay outside this file, each for a stated reason\n# (there were four until 2026-08-11 — see the note below them):\n#\n#   1. `docs/stack.ini` — the PRODUCT TOOLCHAIN, not a process dial. It is\n#      adopter-owned across re-syncs (ADOPTING.md \"Preserve always\"), has five\n#      Python consumers and a working format. Folding it in here would put an\n#      adopter-owned file under a kit-owned template.\n#   2. PRESENCE-AS-SEMANTICS files stay files: `docs/work/pause` (delete-to-\n#      resume IS the contract) and `docs/agents-enabled` (presence = consent to\n#      managed routing). A key in a shared file cannot express deletion-as-an-\n#      act, nor survive as a one-line reviewed diff.\n#   3. `docs/stage` — a GENERATED CACHE (derive_stage.py), not configuration.\n#   (The fourth exception — CHECK-ENABLEMENT TOGGLES stay files, WI-423 — is\n#   OVERTURNED, owner 2026-08-11: \"creating files to toggle something off is\n#   also very confusing … far better to tie those into process.toml and key\n#   them all to on / true.\" The six are the [checks] section at the bottom of\n#   this file now. This repo used to declare none of them, which is precisely\n#   the confusion the ruling names: it looked identical to a repo that had\n#   never heard of the layer. The shipped template's [checks] header carries\n#   the full reasoning, the F5 cost and the legacy fallback's expiry.)\n#\n# MIGRATION: a repo that still carries the legacy one-word files is converted\n# automatically by, from your kept kit checkout,\n# `python project-trajectory/scripts/bootstrap.py --migrate-config --dest .`\n# (bootstrap and the downstream re-sync run it for you). Running with BOTH\n# sources live is REFUSED, not silently resolved — a mixed config is exactly\n# the state where two readers disagree about the same policy.\n#\n# Contracts: IF-037, IF-038 — the interface seams this file declares (process.md\n# §8; rows of record in docs/requirements/interfaces.toml).\n#\n# Contract IF-037: the one home for this repo's policy dials. `[attestation]`\n#     carries the human-approval rung and the review holds, `[policies]` the\n#     push, review-round, privacy and secrets dials, and `[checks]` the\n#     check-enablement toggles. Every value is one `key = value` per line inside a\n#     `[section]`, with no dotted key, inline table or multi-line string, so the\n#     hooks' pure-sh keyed match and the tomllib read see the same declaration; a\n#     shape that breaks that agreement is refused rather than parsed. A dial\n#     resolves to this file first, then to its legacy one-word file for the length\n#     of the migration window, then to the built-in default — and a repo declaring\nwi-798\nwi-902\n028ecf8d6 WI-798: Terra reconciles the change set against the settled code (checkpoint)\nc4fb33e01 WI-798: narrow review 003 of round 4 (APPROVE findings=0)\n9be10a034 WI-798: round 4 (review 002): Gemini and Claude withhold every environment source that outranks the home's login","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"726af005-72ee-4ae5-b3a9-c78cd6ec31fc","uuid":"cf7e072f-d144-4151-8aea-ae73b7355624","timestamp":"2026-10-11T20:53:23.836Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"total 241\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 Peter 197609     0 Oct 11 15:53 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 Peter 197609     0 Oct 11 14:59 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 Peter 197609 18201 Oct 11 14:59 001-REVIEW-A-290c777.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 Peter 197609 10312 Oct 11 15:34 002-REVIEW-A-9752b92-narrow.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 Peter 197609  9328 Oct 11 15:47 003-REVIEW-A-9be10a0-narrow.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 Peter 197609     0 Oct 11 15:53 004-ADJUDICATE-028ecf8.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 Peter 197609    68 Oct 11 15:53 004-ADJUDICATE-028ecf8.md.requested\nbrief = combined\nkinds = amendment;first-approval\noutcome = pending\n0 docs/reviews/wi-798/004-ADJUDICATE-028ecf8.md\n# docs/process.toml — HOW ITEMS ARE PROCESSED. Hand-edited, machine-read.\n#\n# One home for every POLICY DIAL (SN-028). Before this file the dials lived in\n# ~10 one-word files under docs/ (docs/gate-policy, docs/push-policy, …). That\n# idiom was never a ruling — it accreted, and the one constraint that really\n# held it in place was the git hooks' pure-sh parse (repo-review M-42,\n# 2026-07-21: a Python-less box must still FAIL CLOSED on a declared privacy\n# policy). A one-word-per-file format is the only thing a `head -n 1` parse can\n# read; the old 3.8 floor's lack of `tomllib` did the rest.\n#\n# Both reasons are spent. The floor is 3.11 (`tomllib` is stdlib), and the\n# hooks now do a KEYED match instead of a first-line one:\n#\n#     grep -Eq '^[[:space:]]*privacy_check[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*true' docs/process.toml\n#\n# which is why every value below is written ONE `key = value` PER LINE with no\n# inline tables and no multi-line strings. Python consumers read it with\n# `tomllib` and a cross-parser agreement test pins the two readings equal, so\n# the greppable form can never drift from the parsed one.\n#\n# THREE DELIBERATE EXCEPTIONS stay outside this file, each for a stated reason\n# (there were four until 2026-08-11 — see the note below them):\n#\n#   1. `docs/stack.ini` — the PRODUCT TOOLCHAIN, not a process dial. It is\n#      adopter-owned across re-syncs (ADOPTING.md \"Preserve always\"), has five\n#      Python consumers and a working format. Folding it in here would put an\n#      adopter-owned file under a kit-owned template.\n#   2. PRESENCE-AS-SEMANTICS files stay files: `docs/work/pause` (delete-to-\n#      resume IS the contract) and `docs/agents-enabled` (presence = consent to\n#      managed routing). A key in a shared file cannot express deletion-as-an-\n#      act, nor survive as a one-line reviewed diff.\n#   3. `docs/stage` — a GENERATED CACHE (derive_stage.py), not configuration.\n#   (The fourth exception — CHECK-ENABLEMENT TOGGLES stay files, WI-423 — is\n#   OVERTURNED, owner 2026-08-11: \"creating files to toggle something off is\n#   also very confusing … far better to tie those into process.toml and key\n#   them all to on / true.\" The six are the [checks] section at the bottom of\n#   this file now. This repo used to declare none of them, which is precisely\n#   the confusion the ruling names: it looked identical to a repo that had\n#   never heard of the layer. The shipped template's [checks] header carries\n#   the full reasoning, the F5 cost and the legacy fallback's expiry.)\n#\n# MIGRATION: a repo that still carries the legacy one-word files is converted\n# automatically by, from your kept kit checkout,\n# `python project-trajectory/scripts/bootstrap.py --migrate-config --dest .`\n# (bootstrap and the downstream re-sync run it for you). Running with BOTH\n# sources live is REFUSED, not silently resolved — a mixed config is exactly\n# the state where two readers disagree about the same policy.\n#\n# Contracts: IF-037, IF-038 — the interface seams this file declares (process.md\n# §8; rows of record in docs/requirements/interfaces.toml).\n#\n# Contract IF-037: the one home for this repo's policy dials. `[attestation]`\n#     carries the human-approval rung and the review holds, `[policies]` the\n#     push, review-round, privacy and secrets dials, and `[checks]` the\n#     check-enablement toggles. Every value is one `key = value` per line inside a\n#     `[section]`, with no dotted key, inline table or multi-line string, so the\n#     hooks' pure-sh keyed match and the tomllib read see the same declaration; a\n#     shape that breaks that agreement is refused rather than parsed. A dial\n#     resolves to this file first, then to its legacy one-word file for the length\n#     of the migration window, then to the built-in default — and a repo declaring\nwi-798\nwi-902\n028ecf8d6 WI-798: Terra reconciles the change set against the settled code (checkpoint)\nc4fb33e01 WI-798: narrow review 003 of round 4 (APPROVE findings=0)\n9be10a034 WI-798: round 4 (review 002): Gemini and Claude withhold every environment source that outranks the home's login","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_