{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfw855j2CfBfQCHXV9v64","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I held one sitting with two judgements and recorded the verdict in commit `26fbf9100` (trailer `WI: WI-798`). No act commit followed: there were no first approvals to anchor, and the re-anchoring copy was refused.\n\n**Amendment: `VERDICT: MEANING rows=10`.** Every amended row adds an account, a qualifier, a refusal or a prohibition, so none of them is clarity.\n- **Would bless:** LLR-044, LLR-072, LLR-266, LLR-267, LLR-306 and TC-321.\n- **Would not bless; follow-up drafted under `## Dispositions`:**\n  - **LLR-268:** the new sentence says usage mapping \"consumes the environment the call selected\", but `usage(stream)` takes no environment.\n  - **LLR-270:** the LAUNCH clause still describes the per-family home under the store's `home/`, which this work item retires. The code uses the account home, and `HOME_VARIABLES` now also covers opencode and gemini.\n  - **LLR-305:** it says `refuse_competing` applies only to a token account. The code also refuses login accounts that declare a competing credential. The text also says competing names are always removed, but the code removes them only for a declared account.\n  - **TC-324:** its Expected says \"Only a retained launch for a login account\" needs a sign-in. That contradicts its own Method, which still tests token-file refusals, and contradicts LLR-305.\n\n**First approval: `OUTCOME: RETURN rows=7`.** All seven rows go back, with their own drafted follow-up.\n- **LLR-336:** it never states the account credential boundary the code enforces after rounds 3–4. The code keeps every ambient credential that outranks a declared account's home or token out of the launch, and refuses an account whose route or account declares one. Five tests in `tests/test_accounts.py` pin this behaviour and no TC cites them. Its SR-222 parent is unearned, and it repeats rules already stated in LLR-044, LLR-270 and LLR-305.\n- **TC-361 to TC-366:** their Verifies cells claim SRs their tests don't evidence, such as SR-222 for cooldown selection. They also leave out LLRs they are the only check for: LLR-266/267/268/305 on TC-362, LLR-270 on TC-364 and LLR-306 on TC-365. In total, 17 tests in `tests/test_accounts.py` are cited by no TC, and TC-365's Method asserts nothing.\n\n**The act was refused.** I ran `python project-trajectory/scripts/intake.py snapshot --reattests LLR-044,LLR-072,LLR-266,LLR-267,LLR-306,TC-321`, and it exited 1 without writing anything. It refuses because LLR-268, LLR-270, LLR-305 and TC-324 are still drifted in the same two registries. I didn't add them to `--reattests`, since that would anchor text I ruled I would not bless. So the six blessable rows stay drifted, and nothing in the registries or the snapshot has changed. They can be re-anchored in the same act that re-attests the four returned rows once the lane reworks them.\n\nTwo things for the coordinator:\n- **Follow-up drafts:** both are written inside the verdict file, under each section's heading as the sitting asked. I did not add them to WI-798's spec. Because this is a checkpoint on WI-798's own lane, the lane can rework the rows itself before it merges, and the drafts may then be redundant.\n- **`.requested` file:** it is still untracked, because only the verdict file was to be committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":769,"cache_read_input_tokens":205161,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":769},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"726af005-72ee-4ae5-b3a9-c78cd6ec31fc","uuid":"9387fd48-1aa7-4d31-a149-07d1d2af36cc","timestamp":"2026-10-11T21:00:24.750Z","request_id":"re